<.field_report
  signed_in?={@signed_in?}
  title="How ChatGPT Sol, Astra and Terra used emisar to migrate from GKE to bare metal"
  category="Application migration"
  intro="Six application and authentication services moved from Google Kubernetes Engine and Cloud SQL to VA1, a bare-metal Nomad fleet. I used emisar to inspect databases, apply new database infrastructure, and check storage, backups, and replication monitoring. We retired the old GKE clusters while keeping the cloud services the platform still used."
  stats={[
    {"Services migrated", "6"},
    {"Scaling interruption", "39 seconds"},
    {"Old GKE clusters", "Retired"}
  ]}
>
  <.field_report_section
    id="section-1"
    label="Stage 1"
    title="Prepare the applications for Nomad"
  >
    <p>
      I read the application code and deployment configuration, then used the existing Nomad environments in the same infrastructure repository as a reference. I adapted that setup for the applications moving from Google Cloud and prepared their development and production deployments.
    </p>
    <p>
      We deployed these application copies on Nomad while they still connected to Cloud SQL. Public traffic continued to go to Google Cloud. This let us check the new application deployments before moving their databases and traffic.
    </p>
    <p>
      The emisar action <code>gcp.sql_instances</code>
      returned four running PostgreSQL instances across development and production. These were the source databases the applications still used.
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>nomad.job_list_by_meta</code>
      listed the staged application jobs, and <code>nomad.job_status_one</code>
      returned their allocation counts and deployment results. These checks confirmed the destination applications were running before we began the database move.
    </p>
  </.field_report_section>
  <.field_report_section
    id="database-targets"
    label="Stage 2"
    title="Build development databases, then production"
  >
    <p>
      I wrote Terraform changes to create two PostgreSQL targets for development and two for production. These included persistent storage, SQL health checks, backups, and private migration connections to Cloud SQL. I tested the configuration and published separate development and production pull requests in the infrastructure repository.
    </p>
    <p>
      I then used <code>tfc.plan_summary</code>
      to inspect the plans for those changes. Each development and production plan added 26 resources, with no updates, deletions, or replacements. The new targets could be built alongside the existing databases before copying data or switching applications.
    </p>
    <p>
      We brought up the two development targets first. <code>nomad.node_status_all</code>
      returned five ready nodes. <code>nomad.csi_volume_status</code>
      showed that a new PostgreSQL volume could be scheduled and that the storage plugin was healthy on all five nodes.
      <code>consul.service_health</code>
      returned passing TCP and SQL readiness checks.
    </p>
    <p>
      Once the development targets passed their checks, we proceeded with the separate production plan. After approval,
      <code>tfc.apply_run</code>
      queued it. I repeated <code>tfc.run_details</code>
      until it returned <code>applied</code>. Then <code>tfc.create_plan_only_run</code>
      started a follow-up plan, and <code>tfc.run_details</code>
      confirmed that it finished with no changes.
    </p>
    <p>
      A <code>vm.query_instant</code>
      request returned successful full-backup timestamps for all four development and production PostgreSQL targets. The database infrastructure was ready for the initial data copy.
    </p>
  </.field_report_section>
  <.field_report_section
    id="section-2"
    label="Stage 3"
    title="Copy the data and keep it in sync"
  >
    <p>
      Before starting the copy, I used <code>vm.query_instant</code>
      to check the replication watcher's source-query results and last-success timestamps.
    </p>
    <p>
      We copied development data first, then production data. Six application databases lived across the four PostgreSQL targets. The initial copy covered 281 tables; checks found matching row counts, zero replication lag, and no replication errors. Google Cloud remained the writer, and replication kept the new copies current.
    </p>
    <p>
      While replication continued, <code>grafana.alerting_state</code>
      returned alerts for a failing watcher query and a missing or inactive replication slot.
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>nomad.job_allocations</code>
      found the watcher's current allocation. <code>nomad.alloc_status</code>
      showed it running with zero restarts and a healthy deployment. But
      <code>nomad.alloc_logs</code>
      returned repeated records with <code>query_success: 0</code>
      and <code>metrics_pushed: 1</code>. The process was publishing metrics even though its source queries were failing.
    </p>
    <p>
      In one database, a separate check found the existing 46 replicated tables caught up, but four new source tables were missing from the replica. We added their schema, extended the existing publication and subscription, and updated the watcher. Follow-up checks found all 50 tables in that database ready, zero lag, and no active replication alerts. The applications still used Cloud SQL while these copies stayed in sync.
    </p>
  </.field_report_section>
  <.field_report_section
    id="cutover"
    label="Stage 4"
    title="Switch the databases, then public traffic"
  >
    <p>
      On the operator's cutover command, we stopped writers in GKE and the staged Nomad applications, paused scheduled jobs, and synchronized Redis and MQTT broker state.
    </p>
    <p>
      I used the emisar action <code>nomad.job_allocations</code>
      to locate the six replication watchers and <code>nomad.alloc_logs</code>
      to check their progress. All six showed zero lag. We then disabled the PostgreSQL subscriptions and aligned the target sequences so new rows would receive the correct IDs.
    </p>
    <p>
      I switched the application configuration to VA1 PostgreSQL and started the required applications, workers, and schedules.
      The <code>net.http_probe</code>
      checks against VA1 passed before we moved the nine public DNS records. The applications now wrote to the VA1 databases, and public traffic reached Nomad. The old GKE writers remained stopped.
    </p>
    <p>
      After the traffic switch, I updated the database service-discovery templates to supply the current PostgreSQL addresses. Final
      <code>net.http_probe</code>
      checks returned HTTP 200 from development and production web, admin, health, and library endpoints. MQTT checks and scheduled jobs also passed.
    </p>
  </.field_report_section>
  <.field_report_section
    id="section-3"
    label="Stage 5"
    title="Scale the services after cutover"
  >
    <p>
      After cutover was complete, we scaled six application and authentication services to two replicas each, placed on separate nodes. One service moved its sessions into Redis so requests could reach either replica. Two telemetry collectors ran on separate hosts and sent application metrics to VictoriaMetrics.
    </p>
    <p>
      Repeated <code>net.http_probe</code>
      checks recorded a 39-second API 502 window during the first scaling rollout. Both API replicas restarted together after Consul membership changed. I added a two-minute restart stagger, and the corrective rollout stayed healthy throughout the checks.
    </p>
    <p>
      I added a production dashboard with 31 panels. I used <code>vm.query_instant</code>
      to check the live service and exporter metrics. The results showed all six services with two running replicas each, none under-replicated, and two healthy metrics exporters. Repeated
      <code>net.http_probe</code>
      checks covered development and production web, admin, and API endpoints.
    </p>
  </.field_report_section>
  <.field_report_section
    id="section-4"
    label="Stage 6"
    title="Retire the old runtime"
  >
    <p>
      With VA1 serving production, I removed the obsolete GKE delivery path from five application repositories. The old writers and scheduled jobs had remained stopped since cutover.
    </p>
    <p>
      We removed the six disabled replication subscriptions. I used
      <code>postgres.replication_slots</code>
      to check that each publisher's matching slot was gone. The retired slots had been retaining roughly 122 GB of production write-ahead logs. The remaining old application workloads were then scaled down through GitOps.
    </p>
    <p>
      The resource handoff transferred image repositories, media storage and CDNs, CI identities, and release credentials into the Terraform configuration that would remain in use. I compared the resource addresses returned by
      <code>tf.state_list</code>
      for the old and maintained workspaces to check the transfer. The resources themselves stayed in place.
    </p>
    <p>
      We removed migration proxies, stopped the old Cloud SQL instances, and scaled down legacy compute. Both GKE clusters were then deleted. The stopped databases, backups, and rollback disks remained for the final cleanup.
    </p>
  </.field_report_section>
  <.field_report_section
    id="section-5"
    label="Stage 7"
    title="Finish the cleanup"
  >
    <p>
      The final cleanup deleted the stopped Cloud SQL instances, old virtual machines, disks, and load balancers. We also removed obsolete DNS and configuration, preserved the active API uptime check, and archived the old infrastructure and GitOps repositories.
    </p>
  </.field_report_section>
  <.field_report_section
    id="section-6"
    label="The result"
    title="The applications moved and their dependencies stayed available"
  >
    <p>
      Final <code>net.http_probe</code>
      checks returned HTTP 200 from development and production web, admin, API health, and media endpoints. The application services were running on VA1, and the old GKE clusters and migration proxies were gone.
    </p>
    <p>
      Google Cloud retained image delivery, media, monitoring, 76 database backups, and 40 snapshots. The active services had owners in the Terraform configuration that remained in use.
    </p>
    <p>
      After Google's required delay, we finished the private-network cleanup through GitOps, removing the old connections, reserved ranges, and production network.
    </p>
  </.field_report_section>
  <.field_report_section
    id="agent-setup"
    label="Agent setup"
    title="Models and execution environment"
  >
    <table aria-label="Models and execution environment">
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row" class="align-top">Cutover and scaling</th>
          <td>GPT-5.6 Sol <code>(gpt-5.6-sol)</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row" class="align-top">Database setup and final retirement</th>
          <td>GPT-6 Astra <code>(gpt-6-astra)</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row" class="align-top">Replication and deployment repairs</th>
          <td>GPT-5.6 Terra <code>(gpt-5.6-terra)</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row" class="align-top">Reasoning effort</th>
          <td>
            Extra high <code>(xhigh)</code>; Astra used <code>max</code>
            for the final ownership audit
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row" class="align-top">Recorded harness version</th>
          <td>Codex CLI <code>0.148.0</code>, launched from Zed</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row" class="align-top">Sandbox</th>
          <td><.external_link href="https://coop.dryga.com">co:op</.external_link></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row" class="align-top">Infrastructure access</th>
          <td>emisar actions; the sandbox had no direct infrastructure access.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row" class="align-top">Local Codex settings</th>
          <td><code>danger-full-access</code>, approvals <code>never</code> (inside co:op)</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </.field_report_section>
</.field_report>
